\f1\b \cf0 What the war itself costs, as best the sources show
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 US military aid: about $65.9 billion. All US aid: about $127 billion.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Western aid across all categories: about \'80366 billion as of early 2025, and higher now.\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Ukraine's reconstruction needs: about $524 billion over a decade (World Bank, Feb 2025).\
\ls1\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Military casualties: roughly 1.8 to 2 million on both sides, per CSIS, with almost 15,000 verified civilian deaths.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 The cost of negotiating and settling peace
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 I found no published figure. The nearest proxy is the reconstruction estimate above, but that's a cost of rebuilding, not of reaching a deal. In the model, a verified peace is the term that moves V, the part that could bring the barrel price down toward $110. The cost of getting there, talks and diplomacy, is small next to everything else here.\
So the honest bottom line is that the barrel model puts the modeled cost at about $193 per barrel in use if nothing is redeemed, and a verified peace is what would reduce it. The human cost has no per-barrel price, and I wouldn't try to give it one.\

\f1\b The War-Stacked Barrel: A Modeled Effective-Cost Test of the Russian Diesel License
\f0\b0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\i \cf0 \strokec2 Independent analysis, October 20, 2026. Every figure here is a modeled quantity, not a price anyone paid. Inputs marked "unverified" came from the user or a satirical source and need independent checking.
\f0\i0 \strokec2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 1. Question.
\f0\b0  Suppose a government spends heavily to support one side of a war and then eases a sanctions tool that lets the other side sell fuel. What is the effective cost of a barrel in use today if the earlier aid is credited with no value until a peace deal is verified?\

\f1\b 2. Facts the analysis rests on.
\f0\b0 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 OFAC's General License 135 authorizes transactions involving Russian-origin diesel through April 7, 2027. Reporting says buyers, prices, payment and delivery dates were not disclosed.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Bipartisan senators said the license would offer "limited to no relief" to Americans, and Axios reported that analysts doubt the promised relief.\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 US military aid was $65.9 billion per the State Department (January 2025). Kiel puts all US aid near $127 billion (March 2026).\
\ls2\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Moving equipment used fuel. TRANSCOM reported 141,945 tonnes moved by 939 flights, 57 ships, 141 rail shipments and 5,500+ trucks through December 2022. My estimate is about 6 million barrels burned (range 2\'9615 million), roughly 1% of aid cost. This is an estimate scaled from 2022 data, not a measured total.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 \strokec2 3. Model.
\f0\b0  Let V be the value credited to a donated barrel and L = P_donated \uc0\u8722  V the loss. Then 
\f1\b effective cost = P_used + L
\f0\b0 . V is built from a baseline, a ceasefire credit with pre-defined verification rules, and negative terms such as new Russian revenue. It stays at zero until a verified outcome moves it. EIA's WTI averages price the donated barrels at $94.90 (2022), $77.58 (2023), $76.63 (2024) and $65.39 (2025).\

